<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shaykh Junayd</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1460">1460</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Junayd of Ardabil</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shaykh Junayd was head of the Safavid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardabil">Ardabil</a> from 1447 to 1460. Driven from Ardabil by a rival, he turned the order into a militant movement, won followers among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmen">Turkmen</a> tribes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> and married a sister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a>. He was killed in battle against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a> in 1460; his grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a> founded the Safavid state.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shaykh-junayd.jpg" alt="Shaykh Junayd" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gulustan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:02252_020qusar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The ruling house was Sunni Muslim, but it showed favour to Sufi orders, and Uzun Hasan married his sister to <b>Shaykh Junayd</b> and his daughter to Junayd’s son Haydar, leaders of the Safavid order of Ardabil.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Shia Islam spread in their lands through <b>Shaykh Junayd</b> of the Safavid order and the Musha'sha' in Khuzistan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qara-qoyunlu">Qara Qoyunlu · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1460 <b>Shaykh Junayd</b>, the order's militant leader, was killed in battle with the forces of Khalilullah I, and in 1488 his son Shaykh Haydar was killed by Farrukh Yasar, who was aided by the Aq Qoyunlu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/shirvanshah-state">Shirvanshah State · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
